'''tenancy''' (ten′an-si), ''n.'' [< OF. ''tenance'', possession, = Sp. Pg. ''tenencia'' = ML. ''tenentia'', < L. ''tenen(t-)s'', a tenant: see ''tenant''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. In ''law'': (''a'') A holding by private ownership; estate; tenure: as, ''tenancy'' in fee simple; ''tenancy'' in tail. (''b''†) A habitation or dwelling-place held of another.

{{smaller block|The said John Scrips had in like sort divided a Tenement in Shordich into or about seventeene ''Tenancies'' or dwellings, and the same inhabited by divers persons.
{{right|''Proc. in Star Chamber'', an. 40 Queen Elizabeth, quoted in [Ribton-Turner's Vagrants and Vagrancy, p. 123.}}}}

2. The period during which lands or tenements are held or occupied by a tenant.—'''Entire tenancy.''' {{smaller|See ''entire''.}}—'''Estate in joint tenancy.''' {{smaller|See ''estate''.}}—'''Several tenancy.''' {{smaller|See ''entire tenancy''.}}—'''Severance of a joint tenancy.''' {{smaller|See ''severance''.}}—'''Tenancy at will.''' {{smaller|See ''estate at will'', under ''estate''.}}—'''Tenancy by entireties.''' {{smaller|See ''entirety''.}}—'''Tenancy by the courtesy of England.''' {{smaller|See ''courtesy of England'', under ''courtesy''.}}—'''Tenancy from year to year,''' {{smaller|a tenancy which is implied by law sometimes, on the termination of a lease for a year or years and a continuance of the possession without a new agreement.}}—'''Tenancy in common,''' {{smaller|a holding in common with others; an estate consisting in a right to a share of an undivided thing; a tenancy in which all have or are entitled to a common or joint possession, but each has a separate or several title to his undivided share which he can dispose of without affecting the others: distinguished from ''joint tenancy''. See ''estate''. Sometimes called ''coparcenary''.}}

'''tenant'''<sup>1</sup> (ten′ant), ''n.'' [< ME. ''tenant'', ''tenaunt'', < OF. ''tenant'', a tenant, = Pg. It. ''tenente'', a lieutenant, < L. ''tenen(t-)s'', ppr. of ''tenere'', hold, keep, possess. Cf. ''lieutenant''. From the L. ''tenere'' are also ult. E. ''tenable'', ''tenacious'', ''tenacy'',

readings.c1s3:
tempt, temptation'', etc.] 1. In ''law'': (''a'') A person who holds real property by private ownership, by any kind of title, either in fee, for life, for years, or at will. {{smaller|The term is sometimes used in reference to interests in pure personalty, as when we speak of one as ''tenant'' for life of a fund.}} (''b'') More specifically, one who holds under a superior owner, as a lessee or occupant for rent: used thus as correlative to ''landlord''.

{{smaller block|I have been your ''tenant'', and your father's ''tenant'', these fourscore years.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, iv. 1. 14.}}}}

{{smaller block|[The word always implies indirectly the existence of a paramount right, like that of a feudal lord or the modern right of eminent domain. States or nations are not spoken of as tenants of their own property; subjects and citizens are.]}}

(''c'') A defendant in a real action. See ''action'', 8 (''b'').—2. One who has possession of any place; a dweller; an occupant.

{{smaller block|Oh fields! Oh woods! when, when shall I be made
The happy ''tenant'' of your shade?
{{right|''Cowley'', The Wish.}}}}

{{smaller block|The sheepfold here
Pours out its fleecy ''tenants'' o'er the glebe.
{{right|''Cowper'', Task, i. 291.}}}}

3. In ''her.'', same as ''supporter''. {{smaller|A distinction has been made between these terms by alleging that the tenant holds the shield as if keeping it upright, as is usual with modern supporters, but does not support its weight or lift it. (Compare ''supporter''.) Some writers, following the French heralds, use ''tenant'' for a human figure holding or flanking the shield, reserving ''supporter'' for an animal. Also ''tenent''.}}—'''Chief tenant.''' {{smaller|Same as ''tenant in capite''.}}—'''Customary tenant.''' {{smaller|See ''customary freehold'', under ''customary''.}}—'''Kindly tenant.''' {{smaller|See ''kindly''.}}—'''Landlord and Tenant Act.''' {{smaller|See ''landlord''.}}—'''Particular tenant.''' {{smaller|See ''particular''.}}—'''Sole tenant''', {{smaller|one who holds in his own sole}}

