¶
'''tenantable''' (ten′ạn-tạ-bl), ''a.'' [< ''tenant''<sup>1</sup> + ''-able.''] Being in a state of repair suitable for a tenant; that may be tenanted or occupied.

{{smaller block|To apply the distinction to Colchester: all men beheld it as ''tenantable,'' full of fair houses; none as tenable in a hostile way for any long time against a great army.
{{right|''Fuller'', Worthies, Essex, I. 544.}}}}

{{smaller block|He even gave her permission to tenant the house in which she had lived with her husband, as long as it should be ''tenantable.'' {{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, ix.}}}}

'''tenantableness''' (ten′ạn-tạ-bl-nes), ''n.'' The state of being tenantable.

'''tenant-farmer''' (ten′ạnt-fär″mėr), ''n.'' A farmer who is only a tenant, and not the owner of the farm he cultivates.

{{smaller block|We may relieve this country from all responsibility, real or imaginary, for the misfortunes of the Irish ''tenant-farmers.'' {{right|''Nineteenth Century'', XXII. 729.}}}}

'''tenant-farming''' (ten′ạnt-fär″ming), ''n.'' The occupying of a farm on lease, and not as owner.

{{smaller block|''Tenant-farming'' is unprofitable.
{{right|''Edinburgh Rev.'', CLXVI. 301.}}}}

'''tenantless''' (ten′ạnt-les), ''a.'' [< ''tenant''<sup>1</sup> + ''-less.''] Having no tenant; unoccupied; vacant; untenanted.

{{smaller block|Leave not the mansion so long ''tenantless.''
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., v. 4. 8.}}}}

'''tenant-right''' (ten′ạnt-rīt), ''a.'' '''1.''' The right of tenancy of a tenant on a manor, who holds not at the will of the lord but according to the custom of the manor.

{{smaller block|The customary tenants enjoy the ancient custom called ''tenant-right:'' namely, "To have their messuages and tenements to them during their lives, and after their deceases}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|to the eldest issues of their bodies lawfully begotten."
{{right|''H. Hall'', Society in Elizabethan Age, App. I.}}}}

2. The right, or claim of right, in various forms or degrees, on the part of agricultural tenants, particularly in Great Britain and Ireland, to continue the tenancy so long as they pay the rent and act properly, to have the rent not raised so high as to destroy their interest, to be allowed to sell their interest on leaving to a purchaser acceptable to the landlord, and to receive a compensation from the landlord if turned off. {{smaller|The claim last mentioned, recognized as extending to crops left in the ground, labor in preparing the soil for the next crop, produce left on the farm, and of late years the value of permanent improvements, is that more especially known as ''tenant-right.''}}

'''tenantry''' (ten′an-tri), ''n.''; pl. ''tenantries'' (-triz). [< ''tenant'' + ''-ry.''] 1. The condition of being a tenant; tenancy.

{{smaller block|Tenants have taken new leases of their ''tenantries.''
{{right|''Bp. Ridley'', in Dr. Ridley's Life, p. 656. (''Latham.'')}}}}

2. The body of tenants; tenants collectively.

{{smaller block|Yes, Mr. Huxter, yes; a happy ''tenantry'', its country's pride, will assemble in the baronial hall, where the beards will wag all.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Pendennis, lxxv.}}}}

'''tence†''', ''n.'' An obsolete spelling of ''tense''<sup>1</sup>.

'''tench''' (tench), ''n.'' [< ME. ''tenche'', < OF. ''tenche'', F. ''tanche'' = Sp. Pg. ''tenca'' = It. ''tinca'', < LL. ''tinca'', ML. also ''tenca'', a tench.] A cyprinoid fish of Europe, ''Tinca vulgaris.'' {{smaller|It inhabits the streams and lakes of the European continent, and in England it is frequent in ornamental waters and ponds. The fish attains}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|is very tenacious of life, and may be conveyed alive in damp weeds for long distances. The flesh is somewhat coarse and insipid. The tench was formerly supposed to have some healing virtue in the touch. I. Walton ("Complete Angler," p. 175) says: "The Tench . . . is observed to be a Physician to other fishes, . . . and it is said that a Pike will neither devour nor hurt him, because the Pike, being sick or hurt by any accident, is cured by touching the Tench."}}

'''tench-weed''' (tench′wēd), ''n.'' The common pondweed, ''Potamogeton natans:'' so named from some association with the tench (according to Forby, from its coating of mucilage, supposed to be very agreeable to that fish).

'''tend'''<sup>1</sup> (tend), ''v.'' [< ME. *''tenden'', < OF. (and F.) ''tendre'', stretch, stretch out, hold forth, offer, tender, = Pr. ''tendre'' = Sp. Pg. ''tender'' = It. ''tendere'', < L. ''tendere'' (√ ''ten''), stretch, stretch out, extend, spread out, intr. direct one's course, aim, strive, go, tend, = Gr. τείνειν (√ τεν, ταν) = Skt. √ ''tan'', stretch: a root represented in Teut. by ''thin:'' see ''thin''<sup>1</sup>. From the L. ''tendere'' are also ult. E. ''tend''<sup>2</sup>, ''tender''<sup>2</sup> (a doublet of ''tend''<sup>1</sup>), ''tender''<sup>3</sup>, ''tendon'', ''tense''<sup>2</sup>, ''tension'', ''tent''<sup>1</sup>, ''tent''<sup>3</sup>, ''tent''<sup>4</sup>, ''attend'', ''contend'', ''extend'', ''intend'', ''portend'', ''pretend'', ''superintend'', ''contention'', ''extension'', ''intention'', etc.; from the Gr., ''tone''<sup>1</sup>, ''tonic'', ''tune'', etc.] '''I.'''† ''trans.'' To reach out; offer; tender.

{{smaller block|Then Cassivelaunus . . . sent Embassadour to Cæsar by Conius and Arras, ''tending'' unto him a surrendry.
{{right|''Holland'', tr. of Camden, p. 37. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To move or be directed, literally or figuratively; hold a course.

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|If I came alone in the quality of a private person, I must go on foot through the streets, and, because I was a person generally known, might be followed by some one or other, who would discover whither my private visit ''tended,'' besides that those in the inn must needs take notice of my coming in that manner.
{{right|''Lord Herbert of Cherbury,'' Life (ed. Howells), p. 158.}}}}

{{smaller block|See from above the bellying Clouds descend,
And big with some new Wonder this Way ''tend.''
{{right|''Congreve,'' Semele, iii. 8.}}}}

{{smaller block|I know not whither your insinuations would ''tend.''
{{right|''Sheridan,'' The Rivals, iii. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|It further illustrates a very important point, toward which the argument has been for some time ''tending.''
{{right|''J. Fiske,'' Evolutionist, p. 118.}}}}

2. To have a tendency to operate in some particular direction or way; have a bent or inclination to effective action in some particular direction; aim or serve more or less effectively and directly: commonly followed by an infinitive: as, exercise ''tends'' to strengthen the muscles.

{{smaller block|By this time they were got to the Enchanted Ground, where the air naturally ''tended'' to make one drowsy.
{{right|''Bunyan,'' Pilgrim's Progress, ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|To make men governable in this manner, their precepts mainly ''tend'' to break a nationall spirit.
{{right|''Milton,'' Reformation in Eng., ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|No advantage was deemed unwarrantable which could ''tend'' to secure the victory. {{right|''Prescott,'' Ferd. and Isa., ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Natural selection ''tends'' only to make each organic being as perfect as, or slightly more perfect than, the other inhabitants of the same country with which it has to struggle for existence. {{right|''Darwin,'' Origin of Species, p. 197.}}}}

3. To serve, contribute, or conduce in some de-

readings.c3s4:
gree or way; be influential in some direction, or in promoting some purpose or interest; have a more or less direct bearing or effect (upon something).

{{smaller block|Farewell, poor swain! thou art not for my bend;<br>I must have quicker souls, whose words may ''tend''<br>To some free action.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Faithful Shepherdess, i. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|But the place doth not greatly ''tend'' unto tranquility.
{{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 225.}}}}

{{smaller block|All other men, who know what they ask, desire of God that thir doings may ''tend'' to his glory.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, viii.}}}}

{{smaller block|The Spaniard hopes that one Day this Peace may ''tend'' to his Advantage more than all his Wars have done.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, iii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller|=Syn. '''2.''' To incline, lean, verge, trend.—'''3.''' To conduce.}}

'''tend'''<sup>2</sup> (tend), ''v.'' [< ME. ''tenden''; by apheresis from ''attend.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To attend; wait upon as an assistant or protector; guard.
